course: space
lesson: 31
title: Olympiad: planet puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This closing block mixes the whole Solar System into puzzles: each one needs two facts from different lessons joined together, or a trap in the wording spotted before you answer.
explain: Many traps hide in rules that sound obvious: the planet nearest the Sun is not the hottest (Venus is), and the planet furthest out did not have the coldest air ever measured (Uranus did).
explain: Some puzzles are sums with space numbers: Jupiter goes round the Sun once in about 12 years, so a child who has just turned 12 has seen Jupiter make about one lap - two facts, one step.
explain: Picture Ruby from Cardiff at the bus stop, moaning "It took a million years for the bus, I'm tamping" - "a million years" is hyperbole, not a number to count, and "tamping" is Welsh for furious; olympiad puzzles love a big-sounding number that does not count.
explain: Read every word: "meteor" and "meteorite" are different things, "day" and "year" swap their usual order on Venus, and a comet's tails point away from the Sun, not always behind the comet.
fact: From the Sun the planets go Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune; the first four are rocky and the last four are giants.
fact: Venus is the hottest planet, about 465 degrees Celsius, although Mercury is closer to the Sun, because Venus's thick carbon dioxide air traps the heat; Mercury's daytime reaches about 430 degrees.
fact: Uranus has the coldest measured atmosphere of any planet, about minus 224 degrees Celsius, even though Neptune is further out.
fact: Venus takes about 243 Earth days to spin once but only about 225 days to go round the Sun, so one spin takes longer than one lap.
fact: On Mercury one sunrise to the next takes about 176 Earth days, which is two of its 88-day years.
fact: A day on Mars, called a sol, is about 24 hours 40 minutes, so each sol is 40 minutes longer than an Earth day.
fact: Jupiter takes about 12 Earth years to go round the Sun, Saturn about 29.5 years and Neptune about 165 years.
fact: Uranus was found with a telescope in 1781, Neptune was predicted by mathematics and found in 1846, and Pluto was found by comparing photographs in 1930.
fact: Ganymede, a moon of Jupiter, and Titan, a moon of Saturn, are both wider than the planet Mercury.
fact: Gravity on the Moon is about one sixth of the Earth's, so you would feel six times lighter there.
fact: Saturn is less dense than water, so in an impossibly huge bath it would float.
fact: A comet's tails always point away from the Sun, so when a comet is heading back out, its tails go in front of it.
fact: A space rock is a meteoroid in space, a meteor while it glows in the air and a meteorite if a piece lands on the ground.
fact: A solar eclipse can happen only at new Moon and a lunar eclipse only at full Moon; spring tides come at both, so about every two weeks.
fact: "Tamping" is Welsh slang for furious, "howay" is North East English for come on, and "no cap" is young American slang for no lie.
exercise: On Mercury, how many of its own years pass from one sunrise to the next? | 2 | 1 | 88 | 176
exercise: On Venus, which takes longer, and by about how much? | One spin, by about 18 Earth days | One lap of the Sun, by about 18 days | One spin, by about 243 Earth days | Neither: they take exactly as long
exercise: Odd one out: Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter. Which one, and why? | Jupiter: it is a giant, not a rocky planet | Mars: the only one of them with moons | Venus: the only one nearer the Sun | Mercury: the only one with no rings
exercise: Odd one out: Io, Europa, Titan, Ganymede. Which one, and why? | Titan: it goes round Saturn, not Jupiter | Io: it is the only one Galileo discovered | Europa: the only moon here wider than Mercury | Ganymede: the only one with no volcanoes
exercise: A 36-kilogram child stands on the Moon. How heavy would they feel? | As light as 6 kilograms | As light as 30 kilograms | As heavy as 216 kilograms | As light as 12 kilograms
exercise: Neptune was found in 1846 and takes about 165 years per orbit. When will it finish its second orbit since then? | In about 2176 | In about 2011 | In about 2341 | In about 2046
exercise: Kit has just turned 36. About how many times has Jupiter gone round the Sun in his life? | 3 | 36 | 12 | 1
exercise: A rover works for 3 sols on Mars. How much longer is that than 3 Earth days? | 2 hours | 40 minutes | 3 hours | 120 hours
exercise: Put these in order, hottest first: Venus, Mercury's daytime, the Earth. | Venus, Mercury's daytime, Earth | Mercury's daytime, Venus, Earth | Earth, Venus, Mercury's daytime | Venus, Earth, Mercury's daytime
exercise: Which planet had the coldest air ever measured, although it is not the furthest out? | Uranus | Neptune | Mars | Saturn
exercise: A comet is heading away from the Sun after its visit. Where do its tails point? | Ahead of it, away from the Sun | Behind it, back towards the Sun | Straight down, towards the Earth | To the side, wherever space winds blow
exercise: Put in order: a rock flying in space, the same rock glowing in the air, a piece of it lying in a field. | Meteoroid, meteor, meteorite | Meteor, meteoroid, meteorite | Meteorite, meteor, meteoroid | Meteoroid, meteorite, meteor
exercise: Tonight there is a lunar eclipse. About how will the Moon look a week later? | About half lit, near last quarter | Not seen at all, at new Moon | Full again, round and bright | A growing crescent, first quarter
exercise: There was a spring tide today. About when is the next spring tide? | In about two weeks | In about a year | Next March, in spring | Tomorrow
exercise: Which two moons are wider than the planet Mercury? | Ganymede and Titan | Io and Europa | Our Moon and Titan | Phobos and Deimos
exercise: In an impossibly huge bath of water, which planet would float, and why? | Saturn, because it is less dense than water | Jupiter, because it is the biggest planet | Mercury, because it is the smallest | Neptune, as it is mostly water
exercise: Put these discoveries in order: Pluto, Neptune, Uranus. | Uranus, Neptune, Pluto | Neptune, Uranus, Pluto | Pluto, Uranus, Neptune | Uranus, Pluto, Neptune
test: ★★★ Ruby from Cardiff, tamping: "Mercury's closest to the Sun so it's deffo the hottest, end of." What is the truth? | Venus is hotter: its thick air traps the heat | She is right: the closest is always hottest | The Earth is hotter, it has the most air | Mars is hotter, which is why it looks red
test: ★★★ Jayden from Texas: "Y'all, Saturn takes 29 and a half years a lap. Grandpa's 59, so he's seen it go round like a million times." How many laps really? | About 2 | About 59 | About a million | About 29
test: ★★★ Mia from Melbourne: "Venus takes longer to spin once than to go round the Sun, no cap." Is she right? | Yes: a spin is about 243 days, a lap 225 | No: it spins once a day, like the Earth does | No: Venus does not spin round at all | Yes: a spin takes twice as long as a lap
test: ★★★ Grandpa: "Back in my day, a rock that glows across the sky and burns up was called a meteorite, sonny." Put him right. | It is a meteor; a meteorite is a piece that lands | He is right: any glowing space rock is a meteorite | It is a meteoroid all the time it glows | It is a comet, because it glows and has a tail
test: ★★★ A cartoon shows a comet zooming away from the Sun with its tail streaming behind it. What is wrong? | The tails should point away from the Sun, in front | Nothing: a tail always trails behind its comet | Comets heading outwards have no tail at all | The tail should point down towards the Earth
test: ★★★ Ollie from Newcastle: "Howay, Titan's just a moon, so it's gotta be smaller than every planet." Check the claim. | False: Titan is wider than Mercury | True: no moon is wider than a planet | True, but only for Saturn's moons | False: Titan is wider than the Earth
test: ★★★ Zoe says Uranus was found with a telescope, Neptune with maths and Pluto with photographs. Which years match, in that order? | 1781, 1846, 1930 | 1846, 1781, 1930 | 1930, 1846, 1781 | 1781, 1930, 1846
test: ★★★ Ava from Toronto: "Full Moon tonight, eh, so we might get a solar eclipse tomorrow." What is wrong? | A solar eclipse needs a new Moon, about two weeks off | Nothing: solar eclipses come the day after full Moon | Solar eclipses can only ever happen at night | A solar eclipse needs a half Moon, a week off
test: ★★★ Leo, rough: "Shut up, Neptune's furthest out so it's got the coldest air, everyone knows that." What did measuring show? | Uranus's air was measured colder | He is right: Neptune's air is coldest | Mercury's night air is the coldest | Saturn's air is the coldest of all
test: ★★★ Kai from Liverpool weighs 42 kilograms: "On the Moon I'd be dead light, our kid, like 2 kilos." How light would he really feel? | Like 7 kilograms | Like 2 kilograms | Like 36 kilograms | Like 252 kilograms
test: ★★★ Dad: "Mars days are only a smidge longer, nothing really." The rover has worked 10 sols. How much more time is that than 10 Earth days? | 6 hours 40 minutes | 40 minutes | 10 hours | 4 hours
test: ★★★ Nan, being dramatic: "Saturn would float in my bath, it's that light!" What is right? | It is less dense than water, but no real bath could hold it | It weighs less than her bathful of water | It is the lightest of all the planets in mass | It is made only of ice, and ice floats
exam: Determine the number of Mercurian years which elapse between successive sunrises on Mercury. | Two | One | Eighty-eight | One hundred and seventy-six
exam: A comet recedes from the Sun. State the direction in which its tails point. | Away from the Sun, ahead of its motion | Towards the Sun, behind its motion | Across its path, at right angles | Towards the Earth, wherever it lies
exam: Neptune was discovered in 1846 and completes an orbit in about 165 years. State the year in which it completes its second orbit since discovery. | About 2176 | About 2011 | About 2341 | About 2046
exam: Identify the planet in the following list which is not of rocky composition. | Jupiter | Mercury | Venus | Mars
